  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected for \nthe end of next week, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build south of the area through the middle of\nthe week prior to the next low pressure system approaching from\nthe west late this week. With the low centered south of the\narea, the pressure gradient may struggle to relax over the CWA,\nparticularly across the north, and winds could remain breezy\nthrough the middle of the week with increasing winds by Thursday\nafternoon as the pressure gradient increases due to the\napproaching trough of low pressure.\n\nThe timing of the cold front through the end of the week remains\nuncertain, and with the timing uncertainty is also strength\nuncertainty. Generally speaking, the FROPA is expected to cross\nthe area sometime on Friday, though whether that is early Friday\nmorning or late Friday night is still up in the air. The GFS and\nUKMET have the slowest solution at this time, holding the front\nwest of the area until beyond sunset, while the CMC and ICON\nmodels bring the front through around sunrise on Friday. Due to\nthis timing uncertainty, this forecast reflects a blend via the\nNBM, with increasing precip chances through the daylight hours\non Friday. Moistures support with this feature will likely be\nlacking, keeping rainfall showery and limiting rain totals\nthroughout the forecast area, limiting any drought relief\npotential.",
